[libid3tag] Fix latin1 integer overflow on 64-bit Windows

id3_parse_latin1() sizes its copy with malloc(length + 1), and
id3_length_t is unsigned long, which is still 32 bits on Win64. A
compressed ID3v2.3 frame carries a plain 32-bit decoded size, so ~4 MB
of deflated non-NUL bytes in a URL/UFID/PRIV/POPM frame hands it a
length of 0xFFFFFFFF, the + 1 wraps to malloc(0), and the memcpy()
writes 4 GiB past a 1-byte buffer. Tenacity hits this parsing the ID3
chunk of any imported WAV/AIFF. Linux and macOS have a 64-bit long so
they don't wrap, and 32-bit builds can't allocate the decompression
buffer in the first place.

Add id3_util_stralloc(), which does the math in size_t and returns NULL
instead of wrapping, and use it for that allocation. Move the rest of
the (length + 1) * sizeof() allocations in the latin1/utf16/utf8
decoders over to it too; none of those are known to be reachable with
a wrapping length, but there's no reason to leave the pattern around.

Bound the legacy TCON "(nn)" scan in translate_TCON() by the decoded
string's end as well. The NUL terminator already stops it, so behavior
doesn't change.

Reported by Osvaldo Janeri Filho through responsible disclosure.

Signed-off-by: Emily Mabrey <emilymabrey93@gmail.com>

